<!--
The iOS, tvOS and macOS apps' keys the build settings cannot express; Xcode merges the generated ones
(GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE) into this file. It goes through the C preprocessor (apple/project.yml's INFOPLIST_PREPROCESS,
in traditional mode, where two slashes start no comment, so Sparkle's feed URL stays whole; its text never holds a C
comment opener, slash then star): the iOS app defines TORTUGA_IOS and the macOS app TORTUGA_MACOS, so the iOS-only
keys sit under `#if TORTUGA_IOS`, the Mac's under `#if TORTUGA_MACOS`, and those of iPhone, iPad and Apple TV under
`#if !TORTUGA_MACOS`.

App Transport Security: mpv opens streams with FFmpeg's own HTTP stack over BSD sockets, which ATS does not govern,
so plain-HTTP streams play without an exception. The shared core's HTTP client (the Ktor Darwin engine, URLSession)
and the downloads' background URLSession (BackgroundTransport) are governed: NSAllowsLocalNetworking lets them reach
plain-HTTP hosts on the local network only (IP addresses, .local and unqualified names): a self-hosted sync server or
addon at home, and the development addon and media server. Everything on the internet stays HTTPS-only: a plain-HTTP
internet stream can't be downloaded. The shared core holds the sync server to the same rule on every platform, and
refuses plain HTTP to a public IP address too (SyncApi.kt normalizeServerUrl).

Background audio on iPhone and iPad only (TORTUGA_IOS): picture in picture keeps the playback running while the app is
in the background. On tvOS the app is suspended when it leaves the screen. The key is the plain UIBackgroundModes: the
system's picture in picture reads only that one, and device-specific keys (UIBackgroundModes~iphone) left the PiP
button disabled on an iPhone while the simulators showed nothing wrong.

Languages: CFBundleLocalizations lists the interface languages (the files in shared/i18n); it makes the system
offer the per-app language setting (iOS, iPadOS, macOS) and follow the system language (tvOS). The app draws its
strings from the shared core, not from .lproj folders: the app reads the system's language list itself
(AppLaunch) and its own setting in Settings. The system's own prompts read the purpose strings
(NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription, set in apple/project.yml) from <locale>.lproj/InfoPlist.strings, which the build
writes from the same i18n files.

Google sign-in on iPhone, iPad and the Mac (Google's SDK, AppleSignInPlatform): GIDClientID is Tortuga's iOS-type
Google client (project tortuga-510503, bundle com.edde746.tortuga), which the SDK takes on the Mac too, and the URL
type is its reversed client ID, the redirect the SDK signs in with (it refuses to start without the type) and
TortugaApp hands back to it. The Apple TV app doesn't link the SDK (Google signs in there by its device flow), so the
keys go unused on tvOS.

Watch-together invites: the `tortuga` URL type, on iPhone, iPad, Apple TV and the Mac alike, opens
`tortuga://watch?code=XXXXX&relay=<relay URL>` in the app (TortugaApp's onOpenURL), which opens Watch together and
joins the room (RootSession.openUrl). `tortuga://detail?type=<type>&id=<id>`, the Open in Tortuga button of a title
link's page on usetortuga.app, opens that title's Detail. The other `tortuga://` links (a device link's QR code) open
the app and do nothing more, except in a DEBUG build's dev session, where `tortuga://dev-setting?<key>=<value>` changes
a device setting while it runs (AppLaunch, for the UI tests). A title's link itself (`https://usetortuga.app/t/…`)
opens the app as a Universal Link on iPhone, iPad and the Mac (the entitlements' Associated Domains).

Stremio links: the `stremio` URL type, on iPhone, iPad, Apple TV and the Mac alike, opens the links addon sites and
Stremio's pages hand out in the app (TortugaApp's onOpenURL, RootSession.openUrl): an addon's install link
(`stremio://<host>/…/manifest.json`) opens Settings → Addons on that addon's install, and `stremio:///detail/…`,
`/discover`, `/search?search=…`, `/board` and `/library` open those screens.

Player apps on iPhone, iPad and Apple TV (ApplePlayerApps): LSApplicationQueriesSchemes lists the schemes of the player
apps Settings → Player looks for, so that `canOpenURL` can tell whether each is installed (it answers false for any
scheme not listed here): VLC's x-callback-url scheme, Infuse's and SenPlayer's; Apple TV looks for VLC and Infuse only.
Custom players' URL templates open without asking. The Mac finds its player apps by their bundle identifiers
(MacPlayerApps).

Appearance: UIUserInterfaceStyle puts the app in the dark appearance from its launch, on iPhone, iPad and Apple TV
alike. The launch screen the system draws until the app's first frame (UILaunchScreen, generated by the build) takes
the system background colour: white in the light appearance, which showed for the whole start (6 s on a light iPhone
simulator) before the app's black splash, and black in the dark one, the splash's Palette.background. The views ask for
the dark appearance too (tortugaTheme).

Sparkle on the Mac (Updates.swift): the feed is the appcast.xml the release workflow attaches to each
GitHub release (.github/workflows/build.yml), so the latest published release's is the one read. Every update must
carry an EdDSA signature from Tortuga's own update key, whose private seed is the workflow's SPARKLE_PRIVATE_KEY
secret; this is its public key.

The ordered-row type on the Mac (UTType.orderedRow, Design/OrderedRow.swift): what a drag of a reordered list's row
carries, seen by the app's own process alone; declared so the system knows the type the app exports.
-->
